# d3Sea — Muireann on the north headland
#
# BUTTONS ONLY WHERE THERE IS A CHOICE
#   The opening is pure dialogue: her question, the keyboard, her answer,
#   her name. No "I am..." button, because the card already asks. Nodes
#   0-2 carry @continue, so they flow one into the next with the panel
#   standing rather than ending the encounter each time.
#
#   From the hub on, never more than two real options at once. The runtime
#   appends its own exit to any node with buttons, so a third is always
#   there — that is deliberate and cannot be gated away: a card with
#   buttons can't be swiped off, so without an exit the player is trapped.
#
# SILENCE AND LIES ARE INPUT, NOT BUTTONS
#   Refusing is typing nothing and closing the keyboard. Lying is typing
#   something else. Neither needs an option, and neither sets
#   knows_own_name — so the vocative is never invented for a name the
#   roster cannot inflect.
#
# GATING CONSTRAINT
#   requires holds ONE positive note and ONE negative note per option.
#   Chains, never pairs of positives.
#
#     knows_own_name    -> she can say the name properly; opens the password
#     answered_muireann -> her question is behind us; opens the shore
#     knows_fionnbarra  -> opens "Fionnbarra?"
#     knows_of_tollman  -> opens "His hounds?" and "His toll-man?"
#     asked_hounds      -> opens "Which way?"
#
# {playerVoc} ALREADY CARRIES THE PARTICLE. Never write "a {playerVoc}".

@file d3Sea


# ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# No options. The card is her question; dismissing it opens the keyboard.
= node 0 — the meeting
@easca playerName
@eascaMatch knows_own_name
@continue
Who is it that is rowing bravely
% Cé atá ag rámhaíocht go cróga
towards the mouth of the Boyne?
% i dtreo béal na Bóinne?


# ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
= node 1 — she answers, knowing the name
@when note knows_own_name
@continue
Blessing of the shore to you, {playerVoc}.
% Beannacht an chladaigh chugat, {playerVoc}.
Muireann is my name.
% Muireann is ainm dom.
And where are you bound?
% Agus cá bhfuil do thriall?


# ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# A name she cannot place, or none at all. She does not remark on it.
= node 2 — she answers, not knowing the name
@continue
Is that so.
% Ab ea.
Muireann is my name.
% Muireann is ainm dom.
And where are you bound?
% Agus cá bhfuil do thriall?


# ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
= node 3 — the hub
@hold
@again 



# ── her question — TWO OPTIONS ────────────────────────────────────────

* Answer plainly
% Freagair go díreach
@when !note evaded_muireann
@first
@note told_muireann_truth
> To the bog that drinks men whole, to Allen's sunken ground.
% Chun na móna ina gcailltear fir, chuig talamh báite Almhain.
> Where the Fianna test their own.
% Áit a dtriailtear na Fianna.

I’ve seen the bog of Allen,
Who ever saw what I have seen?
I’ve seen the mists that shroud its heart,
How drear and mournful the memory.            
Here, {playerVoc}, the wraiths of the forgotten   
cry out for vengeance amidst the murk and mire.                                            
< Hast thou mastered the spear?
% An bhfuil an tsleá foghlamtha agat?

* Parry 
% Sraon
@when !note told_muireann_truth
@first
@note evaded_muireann
> Ask the heron of the fen. She will know my story.
% Iarr ar chorr na móna. Is aici a bheidh mo scéal.
< Speak plainly. What business brings a stranger to these lands?
% Freagair go díreach mé. Cén gnó a thóg chuig na tailte seo thú?
> The wind does not explain itself to the standing stone.
% Ní mhíníonn an ghaoth í féin don ghallán.
< Some purpose drives thee, I think.
% Tá rún i do chroí dod bhrú ar aghaidh, sílim.

# this isanswering in the negative, truthfully, with a positive spin. There  should be another option. Maybe bragodocious/dishonest; or another parry. 

* Fionn will teach me
% Múinfidh Fionn dom
@when note told_muireann_truth
@first
@note answered_muireann
> Fionn will teach me what the bog does not.
% Múinfidh Fionn dom an rud nach múineann an mhóin.
< I know Fionn, the great one.
% Tá aithne agam ar Fionn, an gaiscíoch mór.
< Fionn keeps poetry before the blade.
% Is uaisle le Fionn an fhilíocht ná an tsleá.

* parry
% sraoin
@when note evaded_muireann
@first
@note answered_muireann
> There is a thing that waits, and it is not yet found.
% Tá rud ag feitheamh orm nach bhfuil feicthe fós.
< Go then.
% Imigh leat mar sin.
< Go where thou art not going.
% Téigh san áit nach bhfuil tú ag dul.

# ── what she knows — ONE, then ONE, then TWO ──────────────────────────

* Where am I?
% Cá bhfuil mé?
@when note answered_muireann
@when !note knows_fionnbarra
@note knows_fionnbarra
> Whose shore is this?
% Cé leis an trá seo?
< Thy little boat has reached the mouth of Bóinn the bright and winding.
% Tháinig do bháidín go béal na Bóinne ghlé ghroí.
< This shore belongs first to her.
% Is léi féin an trá seo ar dtús.
< Fionnbarra mac Dubhloingse is Chief of this valley.
% Is é Fionnbarra mac Dubhloingse Taoiseach an ghleanna seo.

* Fionnbarra?
% Fionnbarra?
@when note knows_fionnbarra
@when !note knows_of_tollman
@note knows_of_tollman
> What kind of man is Fionnbarra?
% Cén cineál duine é Fionnbarra?
< Fionnbarra is not an unjust man.
% Ní fear éagórach é Fionnbarra.
< His hounds are many.
% Is iomaí cú atá aige.
< But his toll-man waits at the river with a face like an angry stone.
% Ach tá a fhear dola ag feitheamh ag an abhainn agus aghaidh mar charraig chrosta air.

* His hounds?
% A chuid con?
@when note knows_of_tollman
@when !note asked_hounds
@note asked_hounds
> Tell me of his hounds.
% Inis dom faoina chuid con.
< Brocán and Dorchán,
% Brocán agus Dorchán,
< and the grey one they call Scáthán.
% agus an ceann liath ar a dtugtar Scáthán.
< It is a dangerous curiosity.
% Is baolach an fhiosracht í.
< Do not think of them,
% Ná smaoinigh orthu,
< lest they hear the thought in thy skull
% ar eagla go gcloisfidís an smaoineamh i do chloigeann
< and come for thee in the darkness.
% agus go dtiocfaidís ort sa dorchadas.

# The password is "Muireann the Druid knows thy name", so it cannot be
# given to someone whose name she does not have. knows_own_name is the
# honest gate: it means she can say it.

* His toll-man?
% A fhear dola?
@when note knows_own_name
@when !note has_druid_word
@note has_druid_word
> His toll-man?
% A fhear dola?
< Such is how he does business:
% Mar seo a dhéanann sé gnó:
< whom he robs not, he delays.
% an té nach ngoidfidh sé uaidh, cuirfidh sé moill air.
< If Fionnbarra's toll-man should meet thee at the strand,
% Má chasann fear dola Fhionnbarra ort ar an gcladach,
< thou art to say that Muireann the Druid knows thy name.
% abair leis go bhfuil d'ainm ag Muireann an Draoi.

* His toll-man?
% A fhear dola?
@when note knows_of_tollman
@when !note knows_own_name
> His toll-man?
% A fhear dola?
< Such is how he does business:
% Mar seo a dhéanann sé gnó:
< whom he robs not, he delays.
% an té nach ngoidfidh sé uaidh, cuirfidh sé moill air.

# ── the road ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Never gated away: a player who has forgotten the way must be able to
# ask again.

* Which way?
% Cén treo?
@when note asked_hounds
@note knows_rath
> Which way, then?
% Cén treo, mar sin?
< Half a morning's walk northwest along the track. Thou wilt see the ringfort.
% Siúil siar ó thuaidh feadh an bhealaigh leath na maidine. Feicfidh tú an ráth.
< Thou wilt find food, shelter, the hospitality of Fionnbarra.
% Gheobhaidh tú bia, dídean, agus fáilte Fhionnbarra ann.
> And after?
% Agus ina dhiaidh sin?
< That great dark place, with water the colour of old bronze.
% An áit mhór dhorcha sin, agus uisce uirthi ar dhath an tsean-chré-umha.
< It is not far. Two days west, two days south. Keep the hills to thy left hand.
% Níl sé i bhfad. Dhá lá siar, dhá lá ó dheas. Coinnigh na cnoic ar do láimh chlé.
< Go not by night.
% Ná téigh de shiúl oíche.
< And if thou hear the sound of pipes where no one stands,
% Agus má chloiseann tú ceol na bpíob gan seinnteoir,
< thou hast heard nothing. Walk on.
% níor chuala tú faic. Lean ort.
< May thy journey succeed.
% Go n-éirí an bóthar leat.

* Leave
% Fág
@exit
@silent
